Step 1 — Create a GitHub Personal Access Token
Go to https://github.com/settings/tokens
Click "Generate new token (classic)"
Give it a name (e.g. bob-mcp)
Select scopes:
repo — full repository access
read:org — org info (optional)
read:user — user info (optional)
Click "Generate token" and copy it — you won't see it again
Step 2 — Confirm Node.js is Installed
Run this in your terminal:

node --version

If not installed, download it from https://nodejs.org. You need v18+.

Step 3 — Locate or Create the MCP Config File
Scope	Path
Global (all workspaces)	C:\Users\<YourName>\.bob\settings\mcp.json
Workspace only	.bob\mcp.json inside your project folder
If the file doesn't exist, create it.

Step 4 — Add the GitHub Server Entry
Open the file and add the github entry under mcpServers. If the file is empty/new:

{
  "mcpServers": {
    "github": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"],
      "env": {
        "GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN": "YOUR_TOKEN_HERE"
      }
    }
  }
}

If the file already has other servers, just add the github block alongside them:

{
  "mcpServers": {
    "sqlite": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-sqlite", "path/to/db"]
    },
    "github": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"],
      "env": {
        "GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN": "YOUR_TOKEN_HERE"
      }
    }
  }
}

Replace YOUR_TOKEN_HERE with the token from Step 1.

Step 5 — Reload Bob
Save the file — Bob hot-reloads MCP configs automatically when a workspace folder is open.
Check the MCP panel in Bob's UI to confirm github shows as ✅ connected.
Step 6 — Test It
Once connected, you can ask Bob things like:

"How many repositories are in my GitHub account?"
"List my open pull requests"
"Create an issue in repo X"
